---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import PageBody from "../components/PageBody.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import TokenTable from "../components/api/TokenTable.astro";
import { defineSections } from "../lib/sections";
import { route } from "../lib/catalogue";

const S = defineSections([
  { id: "anatomy", label: "Anatomy", heading: false },
  { id: "standard", label: "Standard" },
  { id: "connected", label: "Connected" },
  { id: "sizes", label: "Sizes" },
  { id: "semantics", label: "Semantics" },
  { id: "tokens", label: "Tokens" },
]);
---
<DocsLayout page="button_groups">
<PageBody sections={S}>
  <Section {...S.anatomy}>
        <p class="flow-text">
          A button group is a row of related actions that reads as one control
          without becoming one — a formatting row, a set of view actions, a
          player's transport. Each button stays its own button.
        </p>
        <p>
          The root is a <code>&lt;div class="button-group"&gt;</code> and the
          items are ordinary
          <a href={route('buttons')}>buttons</a>, written directly
          inside it — add <code>circle</code> to an item for the icon-only
          shape Material draws a group with most often. There is no plugin and
          nothing to initialize.
        </p>
        <div>
          <div class="button-group">
            <button class="button tonal">
              <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">format_bold</span>
              Bold
            </button>
            <button class="button tonal">
              <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">format_italic</span>
              Italic
            </button>
            <button class="button tonal">
              <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">format_underlined</span>
              Underline
            </button>
          </div>
        </div>
<Code code={`
<div class="button-group">
  <button class="button tonal">
    <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">format_bold</span>
    Bold
  </button>
  <button class="button tonal">
    <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">format_italic</span>
    Italic
  </button>
  <button class="button tonal">
    <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">format_underlined</span>
    Underline
  </button>
</div>
`} />
        <p>
          The group is a container of independent buttons: each one is its own
          Tab stop and does its own thing. It is not a choice — nothing in a
          group is selected. When two to five options answer one question, that
          is a
          <a href={route('segmented_buttons')}>segmented button</a>,
          whose <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> holds the answer.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.standard}>
        <p>
          The default. Every button keeps its own round shape and the gap
          between them closes as they grow — 18dp at the smallest size down to
          8dp at the three largest, which is Material's own ladder. Press one
          and it widens; the buttons beside it move over.
        </p>
        <div>
          <div class="button-group">
            <button class="button circle" aria-label="Previous track">
              <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">skip_previous</span>
            </button>
            <button class="button circle" aria-label="Play">
              <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">play_arrow</span>
            </button>
            <button class="button circle" aria-label="Next track">
              <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">skip_next</span>
            </button>
          </div>
        </div>
<Code code={`
<div class="button-group">
  <button class="button circle" aria-label="Previous track">
    <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">skip_previous</span>
  </button>
  <button class="button circle" aria-label="Play">
    <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">play_arrow</span>
  </button>
  <button class="button circle" aria-label="Next track">
    <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">skip_next</span>
  </button>
</div>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.connected}>
        <p>
          Add <code>connected</code>. The items sit 2dp apart at every size,
          the two ends of the row stay fully round and the joins in the middle
          are squared off. Pressing an item squares its inner corners further
          — 8dp to 4dp at the three smaller sizes, and more at the two large
          ones, where the corners are larger to begin with.
        </p>
        <div>
          <div class="button-group connected">
            <button class="button tonal circle" aria-label="Align left">
              <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">format_align_left</span>
            </button>
            <button class="button tonal circle" aria-label="Align center">
              <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">format_align_center</span>
            </button>
            <button class="button tonal circle" aria-label="Align right">
              <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">format_align_right</span>
            </button>
          </div>
        </div>
<Code code={`
<div class="button-group connected">
  <button class="button tonal circle" aria-label="Align left">
    <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">format_align_left</span>
  </button>
  <button class="button tonal circle" aria-label="Align center">
    <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">format_align_center</span>
  </button>
  <button class="button tonal circle" aria-label="Align right">
    <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">format_align_right</span>
  </button>
</div>
`} />
        <p>
          An <a href={route('icon_buttons')}>icon button</a> is not an
          item. It is its own component — its own size ladder, its own insets
          and its own pressed shape, all set on the element itself — so a group
          can neither size it nor reshape it without fighting rules it already
          has. <code>&lt;button class="button circle"&gt;</code> is the
          icon-only item, and the group sizes it like any other.
        </p>
        <p>
          A connected group has no selected state. Material's connected group
          draws one, and holding it takes a control that remembers the answer
          — which is the
          <a href={route('segmented_buttons')}>segmented button</a>,
          a fieldset of radios or checkboxes. A row of
          <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>s has nothing to read it off.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.sizes}>
        <p>
          The same five sizes as a button — <code>xsmall</code>,
          <code>small</code> (the default), <code>medium</code>,
          <code>large</code>, <code>xlarge</code> — but written once, on the
          group. The group sets the button tokens its items inherit, so the
          buttons inside need no size class of their own; one that carries one
          anyway still wins for itself.
        </p>
        <div>
          <div class="button-group connected xsmall">
            <button class="button tonal">One</button>
            <button class="button tonal">Two</button>
            <button class="button tonal">Three</button>
          </div>
          <div class="button-group connected small">
            <button class="button tonal">One</button>
            <button class="button tonal">Two</button>
            <button class="button tonal">Three</button>
          </div>
          <div class="button-group connected medium">
            <button class="button tonal">One</button>
            <button class="button tonal">Two</button>
            <button class="button tonal">Three</button>
          </div>
          <div class="button-group connected large">
            <button class="button tonal">One</button>
            <button class="button tonal">Two</button>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>
          The same class on a standard group moves its gap too — 18dp at
          <code>xsmall</code>, 12dp at <code>small</code>, 8dp from
          <code>medium</code> up. <code>xlarge</code> is the last rung, 136dp
          tall, for a group that is the only thing on a screen.
        </p>
        <div>
          <div class="button-group xsmall">
            <button class="button tonal">One</button>
            <button class="button tonal">Two</button>
          </div>
          <div class="button-group medium">
            <button class="button tonal">One</button>
            <button class="button tonal">Two</button>
          </div>
        </div>
<Code code={`
<div class="button-group connected medium">
  <button class="button tonal">One</button>
  <button class="button tonal">Two</button>
  <button class="button tonal">Three</button>
</div>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.semantics}>
        <p>
          The group declares no role. A
          <a href="https://github.com/BaezFJ/ExpressiveCSS/blob/master/SEMANTICS.md">composite role</a>
          such as <code>toolbar</code> promises arrow-key navigation, and this
          component <em>rejects</em> it rather than withholding it: its buttons
          are independent commands reached with Tab, so there is no keyboard
          model to implement and none to promise.
        </p>
        <p>
          When the group needs a name — several groups on one screen, or a row
          whose purpose is not obvious from its buttons — give it
          <code>role="group"</code> and an <code>aria-label</code>. That role
          is a grouping and nothing more, so it promises no keyboard contract
          to break.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<div class="button-group" role="group" aria-label="Text style">
  <button class="button circle" aria-label="Bold">
    <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">format_bold</span>
  </button>
  <button class="button circle" aria-label="Italic">
    <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">format_italic</span>
  </button>
</div>
`} />
        <p>
          Items are controls, written as direct children: a
          <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>, or an
          <code>&lt;a class="button" href&gt;</code> when it navigates. A bare
          <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code> is refused — it passes for a control and
          renders as a link, reaching none of the group's corners, press
          behaviour or inherited size. A wrapper element is refused too — the gap
          and the connected corners are written against direct children, so a
          nested <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> loses both. Every icon-only item
          needs its own <code>aria-label</code>, since the icon inside it is
          <code>aria-hidden</code>.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.tokens}>
        <p>
          Set these on the group, or in a rule of your own that picks out the
          groups you want to change. The values below are the defaults — a
          small standard group; the size and variant classes rewrite them.
        </p>
        <TokenTable>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-button-group-between-space</code></td><td><code>12px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-button-group-pressed-item-width-multiplier</code></td><td><code>.15</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-button-group-container-shape</code></td><td><code>9999px</code> (connected)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-button-group-inner-corner-corner-size</code></td><td><code>8px</code> (connected)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-button-group-pressed-inner-corner-corner-size</code></td><td><code>4px</code> (connected)</td></tr>
        </TokenTable>
        <p>
          The items are buttons, so their height, insets and colours come from
          the <a href={route('buttons')}>button</a> tokens, which the
          group's size class sets for them. Material states the press-time
          growth as 15% of the item's width; CSS cannot multiply a length by a
          percentage, so the token holds the same figure as a ratio and the
          growth is taken from the item's height.
        </p>
  </Section></PageBody>
</DocsLayout>
